    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-grimaldi">Francesco Grimaldi</a> captured the fortress on the Rock of Monaco in 1297 with his men disguised as Franciscan friars; after a century of feud with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> the family bought Monaco outright from the Crown of Aragon in 1419, and Honoré II styled himself Prince in 1612.</li><li>In 1861 Charles III ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menton">Menton</a> and Roquebrune, some 95% of the principality's territory, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for 4,100,000 francs in exchange for recognition of Monaco's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-carlo">Monte Carlo</a> casino paid so well that income tax was abolished in 1869.</li><li>Monaco has the world's highest nominal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gross-domestic-product">GDP</a> per head (US$185,742), 31% of its residents are millionaires, and fewer than 10,000 of its 38,423 inhabitants are Monégasque; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> still provides its defence, and in June 2024 the FATF put it under increased monitoring for money laundering.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Monégasque citizens are forbidden to enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monte-carlo">Monte Carlo</a> casino unless they work there, a rule decreed so as not to overtax them; by 1869 the casino's earnings let the principality stop collecting income tax from its citizens altogether, a policy that still stands.</p></aside>
    </section>

